escrow_coordinator.move — Latent Type Audit
============================================

Audit started after PriceState/CreditState extraction (commit 8aaff39).
Refined across multiple waves; each wave exposed residue from the
previous design that the prior wave made visible.

The probe is unchanged:

    "What type would replace this snippet with a match?"

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
THREE KINDS OF if/else SMELL
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Type 1 — Hidden state in branching (extraction of a NEW type)
  Each branch parameterises the SAME operation with different data.
  The body is a function of an implicit value; the value deserves a
  type. Extraction creates a module, condenses the logic to one site.

Type 2 — Predicate-based dispatch on an EXISTING type
  The type that would justify `match` is already defined; the code
  uses `is_*` predicates in if/else instead of pattern matching.
  Refactor is syntactic — replace if/else with `match { | => }`,
  optionally with or-patterns. The win is exhaustiveness.

Type 3 — Control-flow that belongs in a deeper layer
  A boolean (or other flag) is checked multiple times in the same
  body, gating branches that should live inside a lower module.
  The flag is a leak of the lower layer's logic into the consumer.
  Refactor: push the dispatch down; the consumer reads the resolved
  state, not the flag.

Type 1 and Type 3 reduce code size and shift responsibility. Type 2
reduces cognitive load and gives the compiler more to verify.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
META-PATTERNS DISCOVERED ACROSS WAVES
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

PATTERN A — Smell migrates from producer to consumer
  After every successful extraction, audit the *caller* of the new
  abstraction with the same lens. Closure is a system-level claim, not
  a module-level one. The smell doesn't disappear — it migrates.

PATTERN B — Intermediate abstraction absorbs imports
  When a coordinator calls module M to produce a value V, then calls
  modules A, B, C on V's components — and M already imports A, B, C —
  the operations belong inside M. Discovered via:
    RefundState::distribute  (absorbed owner, fee_message, tenant)
    lifecycle_state::take_asset  (absorbed asset_state, tenant_state)
    refund_state::from_departing  (absorbed Parcial/Nothing choice)
  Rule: if M already imports A and the coordinator calls A-on-M-value,
  push the call into M.

PATTERN C — Coordinator should observe results, not implement logic
  If the coordinator reads internal flags from lifecycle_state to make
  decisions AFTER a transition, those decisions belong in the transition
  itself. The coordinator should observe the resulting state, not
  re-derive consequences from a flag that leaked across the boundary.
  Closed by: absorbing `retiring` into expire_tenure (Type 3).

PATTERN D — Config abstraction (decided NOT to extract)
  config::tenure_ceiling + phases::has_passed etc. looks like Pattern B
  but is structurally different: the computations require BOTH a state
  value (phase_start, from lifecycle_state) AND a config value (policy).
  The coordinator owns both, so this is legitimate coordination, not
  smell. `phases` is not imported by config at all — adding it would be
  a new dependency without structural justification. Verdict: leave as is.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
MOVE 2024 LANGUAGE CONSTRAINT — EXTERNAL MATCH FORBIDDEN
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Move 2024 splits enum visibility into two levels:

  · The TYPE is public — any module can hold a value of the type.
  · The VARIANTS are private to the defining module — only the defining
    module can pattern match on them (construct, read, unpack).

Source: The Move Book, "Enums and Match":
  "Enums are internal to the module in which they are defined. This
   means an enum can only be constructed, read, and unpacked within
   the same module."

  "With the addition of is_variant functions, we enabled external
   modules to check which variant an enum instance represents.
   However, external code still cannot access the inner value of a
   variant due to enums being internal to their module."

This is not a limitation — it is the language enforcing the same
module-boundary principle we have been applying manually:

  "If you have logic that dispatches on variants, that logic belongs
   in the module that defines the variants."

CONSEQUENCE for this codebase:
  · if/else over is_a_state_* predicates in the coordinator is the
    CORRECT pattern — it is how an external module consumes an opaque
    type whose variants it cannot see.
  · Every Type 2 smell we could not close ("blocked by Move") is
    actually the language correctly requiring that dispatch logic live
    inside lifecycle_state, pending_transition, etc.
  · The is_variant + predicate API (is_a_state_idle, is_handover,
    is_stale…) IS the canonical Move 2024 interface for external
    consumers of an enum.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
CLOSED — REFACTORS EXECUTED (HEAD = 2d96b96)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

✅ EscrowStateTag — eliminated entirely (commit 7fea24f)
   EscrowStateTag was a parallel type maintained for event fields and
   external tag-dispatch — a projection of LifecycleState that lived in
   the coordinator. Replaced with five direct predicates on
   EscrowCoordinator (is_idle, is_at_dutch_auction, is_handover_open,
   is_handover_confirmed, is_retired) forwarding to lifecycle_state.
   Event fields that carried the tag were removed (derivable from event
   sequence). −104 lines net.

✅ RefundState::distribute — TYPE 1 (commit 5a7a960)
   consume_nothing / consume_parcial / consume_total + has_remainder
   removed. Single distribute(rs, &mut owner, fee_inbox_id, displaced,
   ctx) with exhaustive match inside refund_state. Coordinator call sites
   in do_handover, do_tenure_expiry, do_supersede_bid each collapsed to
   one line. Also absorbed: fee_message and tenant imports eliminated
   from coordinator.

✅ lifecycle_state::take_asset — PATTERN B (commit 5f44c3a)
   decompose_retired returned (AssetState, TenantState) and coordinator
   called asset_state::claim + tenant_state::consume_absence on them.
   lifecycle_state already imported both. take_asset absorbs both ops;
   coordinator's claim_asset collapses to one line. Eliminates
   asset_state and tenant_state from coordinator imports.

✅ do_tenure_expiry retiring branch — TYPE 3 (commit ba75c2d)
   was_retiring local read before expire_tenure, used in two if-blocks
   after it (second take/put cycle + AssetRetired event). retire flag
   is internal to lifecycle_state. expire_tenure now consumes retiring
   directly: chains asset_state::expire → asset_state::retire when
   retiring=true. Coordinator checks is_retired(escrow) instead of the
   propagated boolean. Second take/put cycle eliminated.

✅ CapAuthorization — TYPE 1, own module (commit 14ea4d8)
   borrow_asset and burn_tenant_cap each implemented multi-field
   lifecycle_state queries to classify "what role does this cap play?"
   New module cap_authorization.move (mirrors pending_transition.move):
   enum CapAuthorization { Current, Pending, Stale } with public(package)
   constructors and public predicates. lifecycle_state::cap_authorization
   owns the classification; coordinator observes result and aborts with
   its own error codes.

✅ refund_state::from_departing — PATTERN B (commit 53b29a5)
   accept_bid in lifecycle_state had if (stake_value > 0) to choose
   between refund_state::parcial and refund_state::nothing. Both branches
   called tenant::unbundle. refund_state already imports tenant. The
   Parcial/Nothing choice belongs in the defining module. Call site
   collapses to one line.

✅ curve_shape integer dispatches — TYPE 2 (commit 2d96b96)
   eval_power_law dispatched on alpha_den ∈ {2,3,4} with if/else.
   exp_a_norm dispatched on (alpha_abs ∈ 1..8, alpha_neg) with 16-case
   nested if/else. Move 2024 supports match on integer literals and bool.
   Both converted to match expressions (tuple patterns not supported;
   exp_a_norm uses match(alpha_neg) { match(alpha_abs) }).

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
HONEST DISPATCH — NOT SMELL (verified clean)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

A. rent / retire / fire dispatchers (coordinator)
   if/else over is_idle, is_at_dutch_auction, is_handover_open etc.
   These cannot be match expressions: LifecycleState variants are
   private to lifecycle_state (Move 2024 constraint). The predicates
   ARE the canonical external interface. This is honest dispatch —
   the language requires it.

B. compute_floor_price / compute_used_credit (coordinator)
   if/else over lifecycle_state predicates to derive PriceState /
   CreditState regime. This IS the regime derivation step: extracting
   parameters from lifecycle_state to construct the correct regime
   variant. Legitimate work, not smell. The arithmetic moved into
   price_state / credit_state; the parameter extraction stays here.
   (Also blocked by Move constraint — same as A.)

C. next_pending (coordinator)
   Three if-blocks over lifecycle_state predicates + config values.
   Needs BOTH state (phase_start, expiry) AND config (tenure_ceiling,
   descent policy). The coordinator owns both. Cannot push to either
   lifecycle_state (no config) or config (no state). Genuine
   coordination logic, not a leak.

D. is_retiring guard in do_place_bid
   assert!(!lifecycle_state::is_retiring(s), ERetireFlagBlocksBid)
   The coordinator OBSERVES a public predicate and enforces a business
   rule with its own error code. This is different from was_retiring
   (which implemented a state transition consequence). Correct pattern.

E. apt_step Option dispatch
   if (option::is_some(&pending)) { fire } else { destroy_none }
   Idiomatic Option handling. No domain type hiding.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
OPEN QUESTION — PRE-READING BEFORE MUTATIONS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

do_handover and do_tenure_expiry read lifecycle_state fields BEFORE the
transition to capture data for events:

  do_handover:      current_addr, current_stake_value (before accept_bid)
  do_tenure_expiry: current_addr, current_stake_value (before expire_tenure)

These values are also known internally to accept_bid / expire_tenure
before they mutate the state. If the transition functions returned
them as output, the coordinator wouldn't need to pre-read internal state.

Candidate shape:
  accept_bid   → (LifecycleState, RefundState, displaced_addr, principal)
  expire_tenure → (LifecycleState, RefundState, tenant_addr, principal)

Risk: larger API change; gain is modest (removes two reads per call site).
Verdict: DEFERRED — not currently worth the API surface expansion.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
TEST PROTOCOL
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The 429-test suite is the safety net. Each extraction is one commit
that must keep all tests green.

A test failure is diagnostic in two ways:
  1. The abstraction did not preserve observable behavior.
  2. A test was verifying the OLD implementation shape, not the
     semantic contract. In that case the test must be updated to assert
     the new contract — not silenced. Example: set_retiring_lifts_flag
     was asserting "expire_tenure produces AtDutch when retiring=true"
     (old shape). Updated to assert "expire_tenure produces Retired
     when retiring=true" (new semantic contract).

Distinguishing (1) from (2): if the protocol-level observable behavior
is preserved (same final state, same events emitted, same fund routing)
and only the internal step count changed, it is (2).
